        <h2 id="why-it-fails" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Why Paint Fails on Cinder Block
        </h2>

        <p>
          Concrete block presents four distinct problems all at once, and ordinary wall paint is designed to handle none of them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It is extremely porous.</strong> The face of a standard block is riddled with open voids — you can see them without magnification. Paint applied directly runs into those voids, so a coat that would cover a hundred and fifty square feet of drywall covers perhaps sixty of block, and still leaves pinholes across the whole surface.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It is alkaline.</strong> Portland cement is strongly alkaline, and that alkalinity attacks conventional paint binders in a process called saponification — the binder is chemically broken down, the colour shifts, the film chalks, and adhesion goes. New masonry is worst, but even old block has enough residual alkalinity to matter.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It moves.</strong> Masonry walls expand and contract with temperature, and block walls develop hairline cracks along mortar joints as a matter of course. A rigid coating cannot follow that movement and cracks with it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It transmits moisture.</strong> Block behaves as a wick. Water sitting in the ground behind a basement wall, or driving rain landing on an exterior one, travels straight through the block and emerges at the painted face. When it does, it pushes the coating off from behind — which is why blistering paint on masonry is nearly always a moisture problem rather than a paint problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          A masonry system solves all four: a filler for the porosity, alkali-resistant chemistry, an acrylic binder for flexibility, and a permeability appropriate to how wet the wall is. Miss any one and the wall fails at that point.
        </p>

        <h2 id="block-filler" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Block Filler Is Not Optional
        </h2>

        <p>
          If you take one thing from this page, take this. The most common reason a painted block wall looks disappointing is that nobody filled it first.
        </p>

        <p>
          A block filler — sometimes sold as a block surfacer — is a very high-solids primer whose purpose is to bridge and fill the open pores in the block face rather than merely to seal it. It goes on thick, it is hard work to roll, and it turns a pitted surface into a continuous one.
        </p>

        <p>
          A standard primer cannot do this. It is formulated to be thin enough to penetrate and promote adhesion, so on block it soaks into the voids and leaves them exactly as open as they were. The topcoat then has to bridge those voids on its own, which it cannot, and the result is a wall covered in tiny pinholes that collect dirt and look unfinished from any distance.
        </p>

        <p>
          The economics also favour filling. Block filler is cheaper per gallon than good topcoat, and it dramatically reduces how much topcoat the wall absorbs. Filling first typically means two coats of finish paint instead of three or four, so the extra step frequently pays for itself outright.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is worth noting that block filler can be tinted towards your topcoat colour at most paint counters, which costs nothing and makes the finish coats cover more evenly — particularly when going to a dark colour over grey block, where an untinted white filler will otherwise show through at every high point.
        </p>

        <p>
          On standard smooth-face block, one coat of filler is usually enough. On split-face, textured or particularly coarse block, plan for two. Roll it with a three-quarter inch or one-inch nap and work it into the surface rather than laying it on top — back-rolling every section while it is wet is what actually drives it into the voids.
        </p>


