        <h2 id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Deal With Moisture First
        </h2>

        <p>
          On a basement or below-grade block wall, this section matters more than the paint choice. Coatings applied over an active moisture problem fail, and they fail in ways that make the eventual repair worse.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start by identifying whether the wall is genuinely wet or merely condensing. Tape a square of plastic sheet tightly to the block and leave it for two days. Moisture on the underside, against the block, means water is coming through the wall. Moisture on the room side means humid air is condensing on cold masonry — an entirely different problem solved with ventilation and dehumidification rather than with coatings.
        </p>

        <p>
          If water is coming through, look outside before reaching for a product. Gutters discharging at the foundation, ground sloping towards the house, and blocked or missing drainage account for the substantial majority of wet basement walls, and all three are cheaper to fix than any coating system. A waterproofing paint applied to a wall with a downspout emptying against it outside is a delaying tactic.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Efflorescence — the white chalky bloom — is a diagnostic sign rather than a cosmetic one.</strong> It is mineral salt left behind by water that has travelled through the block and evaporated at the surface. Its presence is proof that water is actively moving through the wall. Brush it off and it will come back until the water stops.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is also a real argument against sealing damp block tightly from the inside. Water that cannot evaporate at the interior face stays in the block, and in a cold climate it freezes there. Freeze-thaw cycling spalls the face of the block, and when the coating eventually lets go it brings the surface of the masonry with it. Where a wall is genuinely wet and cannot be fixed from outside, a breathable finish that lets the wall dry inward is often the wiser compromise.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interior Block vs Exterior Block</h3>

        <p>
          The two cases pull in opposite directions and it is worth being explicit about which one you are solving. An <strong>exterior</strong> block wall is exposed to driving rain, UV and a full seasonal temperature swing. Flexibility and UV stability matter most, which is why elastomeric coatings earn their place there — they bridge the hairline cracking that any block wall develops along its mortar joints, and they keep bridging it as the wall moves.
        </p>

        <p>
          An <strong>interior</strong> below-grade wall faces no UV and very little temperature movement, but it does face moisture arriving from behind under pressure. Here flexibility matters little and permeability matters a great deal. A thick, impermeable elastomeric coating applied to the inside of a damp basement wall is close to the worst option available, because it seals the one face through which the block could otherwise dry.
        </p>

        <p>
          A garage or workshop wall above grade sits between the two and is the easiest case of all — no hydrostatic pressure, no UV, and usually dry block. That is where a straightforward filler and acrylic topcoat performs best and costs least.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Preparation Step by Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Let new block cure.</strong> A minimum of 28 days, longer in cool or damp weather. New masonry is at its most alkaline and painting early guarantees alkali burn.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Remove everything loose.</strong> Wire brush the whole surface. Old flaking paint, laitance, dust and mortar smears all have to go — the coating can only be as well attached as what it is attached to.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Deal with efflorescence properly.</strong> Brush it off dry first, then wash with a masonry cleaner if deposits remain. Do not paint over it, and treat its reappearance as evidence the moisture source is still active.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Repair cracks and voids.</strong> Hairline cracks can be bridged by an elastomeric coating; anything wider than about a sixteenth of an inch wants hydraulic cement or a masonry patching compound first. Repoint failed mortar joints before painting rather than after.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wash and rinse.</strong> A pressure washer on a low setting works well outdoors; a stiff brush and detergent indoors. Rinse thoroughly, because detergent residue interferes with adhesion.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry completely.</strong> Block holds water far longer than the surface suggests — allow several days after washing, and longer in humid conditions. This is the step most often rushed.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Test the alkalinity if the block is newer than a few months.</strong> Inexpensive pH strips wetted against the surface will tell you. Above roughly pH 10, wait longer or use a coating specifically rated for high-alkalinity masonry.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Application and Coverage
        </h2>

        <p>
          Budget for far more paint than the tin implies. A block wall's real surface area is several times its measured area once you account for the texture and the open pores, and coverage figures on the label are quoted for smooth substrates. As a working rule, expect a block filler to cover around 75 to 100 square feet per gallon, and a topcoat over filled block to cover perhaps 250 — well below the 400 the tin will claim.
        </p>

        <p>
          Use a heavy nap roller — three-quarter inch as a minimum, one inch on rough block — and a brush for mortar joints and corners. The roller carries material into the recesses; the brush is what gets it into the joints, which are the deepest part of the surface and the first place a thin coat shows.
        </p>

        <p>
          On the first coat of any filler or waterproofer, work the material into the surface rather than spreading it across. Some manufacturers specify brush application for the first coat for exactly this reason, and where they do, the warranty usually depends on it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Respect the recoat intervals. Thick masonry coatings hold solvent and water for longer than their surface suggests, and recoating a filler before it has properly dried traps moisture that will later blister the topcoat. When the label says four hours and the room is cold and damp, give it a day.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, paint in reasonable conditions. Most masonry coatings want a surface temperature above 50°F and falling humidity, and none of them should be applied to a wall that is about to freeze overnight. On exterior work, follow the shade around the building rather than working in direct sun, which skins the surface before the coating can penetrate.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping block filler</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ordinary paint cannot bridge the open pores in a block face. Without filler you get pinholes across the whole wall, you use three times the paint, and the finish still looks rough.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Painting new block too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Fresh masonry and mortar are strongly alkaline and stay that way for weeks. Paint applied before the wall has cured gets chemically burned — colour shifts, adhesion fails, and the coating comes off in sheets.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Painting over active moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A coating cannot hold back water that is being pushed through the wall. Find the source, fix the drainage, and only then paint — otherwise the film blisters off and takes the effort with it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using interior wall paint outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Standard latex has neither the flexibility for masonry movement nor the alkali resistance for cement. On an exterior block wall it will craze and peel within two seasons.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing damp block with a waterproof film on the inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Trapping moisture inside the block does not stop it — it moves the failure point. The water freezes, spalls the face, and the coating takes the surface of the block with it when it goes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of paint do you use on cinder block?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A 100 per cent acrylic masonry paint over a masonry block filler primer. The filler bridges the open pores and creates a continuous surface; the acrylic topcoat provides colour, flexibility and alkali resistance. Ordinary interior latex on bare block will pinhole and peel.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to prime cinder block before painting?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, and specifically with a block filler rather than a standard primer. Block filler is a high-solids product designed to fill the porous voids in the block face. A thin primer soaks straight in and does nothing to solve the texture.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint cinder block basement walls?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, provided the wall is dry and any moisture source has been dealt with. Where there is active seepage, a waterproofing coating rated for hydrostatic pressure is the correct product, and even that is a management measure rather than a fix for a drainage problem.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should new cinder block cure before painting?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">At least 28 days, and longer in cool or damp conditions. New masonry is highly alkaline and needs time for that alkalinity to drop. Painting early causes alkali burn — colour change, chalking and adhesion failure that cannot be repaired without stripping.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of paint on cinder block?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">One coat of block filler and two of topcoat is the standard specification. Heavily textured or split-face block frequently needs two coats of filler. Expect coverage to be roughly half what the paint tin claims, because the surface area is far greater than it looks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is elastomeric paint and do I need it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It is a thick, highly flexible coating that bridges hairline cracks and moves with the wall. It is genuinely useful on exterior block that cracks seasonally. Indoors it is usually unnecessary, and on a damp wall its low permeability can trap moisture.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over efflorescence?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. Efflorescence is salt deposited by water moving through the block, and paint applied over it has nothing solid to bond to. Remove it with a stiff brush or a masonry cleaner, then establish where the water is coming from — its presence means the wall is wet.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal or paint cinder block?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Seal if you want to control moisture and are not concerned with appearance; paint if you want colour and a finished look. On a basement wall many people need both — a penetrating sealer to manage moisture, then filler and paint for the finish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

